r more let's bring in our  correspondent charlotte chelsea pill in  london charlotte 150 000 lives lost to  covet 19. how are people reacting to  this  clearly a very somber milestone here in  the uk and of course there are different  ways of measuring this death toll one  suggesting that it could in fact be even  higher  clearly extremely uh tragic this  milestone now now the prime minister has  released a statement on twitter he said  that coronavirus has taken what he  called a terrible toll on our country he  said each and every one of those who  died as a profound loss to the families  friends and communities affected and he  took the moment to really stress how  getting the booster jab getting the  first and second vaccine is really the  path out of this pandemic now there has  of course been criticism over through  the course of the pandemic over the  government's handling of this crisis and  a spokesperson for the campaign group  bereaved families for justice which  supports families who've lost loved ones  to covert has been very critical of this  milestone they have said that  public inquiry into the government's  handling of the pandemic cannot come  soon enough  all of this questioning charlotte as the  country is also combatting a wave of  omicron infections how are hospitals  coping  there's no doubt that the health service  here is under immense pressure at the  moment  hospitalizations and fatalities have  started  rising here and what's very clear is  they were already dealing with a backlog  the effects of months of this pandemic  with hospitalizations now rising winter  pressures and crucially staff absences  fueled by this omicron variant this is a  critical time for the health service  at the moment as you mentioned earlier  here in london the military 200 military  personnel have been sent in to try and  support the health service although of  course that will do  will not compensate for the sheer number  of staff who are absent or isolating at